{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01S7pDQ1GhXHWwuWznzxFo3G","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Wrote **`ai-engineering/answers/01-018-explain-kl-divergence.md`** — Q18: Explain KL divergence.\n\nKey content in the note:\n\n- **Concept:** KL(P‖Q) = Σ P(x)·log(P(x)/Q(x)) — extra bits to encode samples from P using a code built for Q; not a symmetric distance metric\n- **Asymmetry:** Forward KL (mode-covering) vs reverse KL (mode-seeking) — and why the direction chosen in each context is deliberate\n- **Where it shows up in LLMs:**\n  - RLHF/PPO: `max E[r] − β·KL(π_new ‖ π_ref)` — the KL term is the leash preventing reward hacking; β ≈ 0.1–0.5\n  - DPO (Rafailov 2023): reformulates the same KL-constrained objective into a closed-form loss over preference pairs — no separate reward model needed\n  - VAEs, label smoothing, calibration measurement\n- **Pitfalls:** calling it a \"distance metric\" (it's not symmetric); not connecting it to RLHF/DPO in context; conflating cross-entropy with KL\n- **Cross-links:** Q29 (RLHF vs DPO), Q4 (pre-training vs fine-tuning), Q6 (scaling laws)"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":340,"cache_read_input_tokens":56221,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":340,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7842c1b-c4e4-471a-a18e-0b86c80f9c21","uuid":"46761dc1-9b68-42e7-bb58-43c5fd0c9303"}
